WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.450 --> 00:00:08.290
And Finson was a tall, Lincoln-esque figure at the NIH who reached for his viola

2
00:00:08.290 --> 00:00:09.830
when the science got too loud.

3
00:00:10.633 --> 00:00:16.653
In 1960 he took ribonuclease apart with urea and beta-mercaptoethanol — a reagent

4
00:00:16.653 --> 00:00:21.433
that, the book notes, smells like rotten eggs crossed with skunk and watched the

5
00:00:21.433 --> 00:00:24.753
enzyme collapse to zero activity, a random coil.

6
00:00:25.633 --> 00:00:27.993
Then he washed the chemicals away and waited.

7
00:00:28.613 --> 00:00:33.753
The molecule found its way back to 100% activity, the molecular equivalent of an

8
00:00:33.753 --> 00:00:35.333
egg unscrambling itself.

9
00:00:35.893 --> 00:00:39.753
No template, no helper, no instructions but its own sequence.

10
00:00:40.667 --> 00:00:46.327
He stated it plainly to the Royal Swedish Academy in his 1972 Nobel lecture: the

11
00:00:46.327 --> 00:00:50.907
native fold is the lowest free energy state the sequence can reach, in a given

12
00:00:50.907 --> 00:00:51.907
environment.

13
00:00:52.400 --> 00:00:56.780
Years later he left the lab and flew to Argentina during the Dirty War to confront

14
00:00:56.780 --> 00:00:59.300
government ministers over disappeared scientists.

15
00:01:00.060 --> 00:01:05.080
His method, the book observes, was the same one he had used on the enzyme: remove

16
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:07.500
the denaturant, and see what refolds.
